{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc | sed -n '1,280p;640,770p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t(ns yin.vm.ucf.remote\n     2\t  \"The UCF facade through `dao.stream.remote` descriptors: lifts a\n     3\t   parked semantic machine's pending wait\n     4\t   (`docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` S7.4.3) into\n     5\t   UCF data whose cells and stream markers name `dao.stream.remote`\n     6\t   descriptors under the `:dao.stream.remote/v1` cursor profile\n     7\t   (S7.5.3), and lowers that data back into a resumable local wait\n     8\t   through a reflection (`docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md` S2.2,\n     9\t   S2.4).\n    10\t\n    11\t   The local side is the engine's own parked machinery, not an\n    12\t   invented shape. A pending entry is what `yin.vm` parks: the\n    13\t   machine registers beside `:stream-id` and `:cursor-ref`\n    14\t   (`yin.vm.semantic` S3.5's park entries, `response-wait-entry`;\n    15\t   `yin.vm.engine`'s wait-set sweep), every handle and cursor\n    16\t   resolved out of the machine's private `:resources` table -- the\n    17\t   lift reads the table, and no entry ever carries a live handle.\n    18\t   The lower mirrors it: fresh resource entries, one per stream\n    19\t   reflection and one per cursor cell (two waiters sharing one cell\n    20\t   share the one entry, S7.5.3), and wait-set entries that merge the\n    21\t   caller's register context verbatim, so a lowered wait parks under\n    22\t   the engine's own sweep exactly as an unmigrated one does.\n    23\t\n    24\t   Where the engine polls a wait at one of its own fixed resource\n    25\t   keys, the lower parks the wait there: a link wait's pair under\n    26\t   `yin.vm.module`'s link-request-resource and\n    27\t   link-response-resource, reflections and entry fields alike, and a\n    28\t   retained request as the engine's own `:request-sent` shape on the\n    29\t   fixed local call-in key -- the receiver's own pair serves the\n    30\t   retried request and the restored response wait reads the\n    31\t   receiver's own call-out, so the fixed keys serve the resumed\n    32\t   program's calls alone. Every response route travels as its cell\n    33\t   and lowers under the cell's own fresh key (S7.5.3) -- distinct\n    34\t   cells stay distinct, shared cells stay shared -- never at a fixed\n    35\t   key, so a migrated route can never misroute a new call.\n    36\t\n    37\t   `lift-pending` and `lower-pending` are plain-data functions,\n    38\t   parameterized by two composition callbacks rather than coupled to\n    39\t   `yin.vm` or `dao.stream.remote` internals:\n    40\t\n    41\t     serve!  : handle -> {:dao.stream/identity id\n    42\t                          :dao.stream/channel chan} | nil\n    43\t               the exporter's own table entry\n    44\t               (`dao.stream.remote.md` S2.3): nil when the handle\n    45\t               cannot be served -- no table to enter it in, no\n    46\t               channel to the resumer -- which refuses the lift as\n    47\t               `:yin.k/unsatisfied` before minting the value\n    48\t               (S7.4.3).\n    49\t\n    50\t     attach! : remote-descriptor -> {:dao.stream/outcome\n    51\t                                     :dao.stream/ok\n    52\t                                     :dao.stream/handle reflection}\n    53\t               | outcome-map\n    54\t               the receiver's own `:dao.stream/attach` dispatch for\n    55\t               `:dao.stream/remote` (`dao.stream.remote.md` S2.4): an\n    56\t               outcome other than `ok` -- including a reflection\n    57\t               already marked gone -- refuses the lower as\n    58\t               `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream identity.\n    59\t\n    60\t   The `:dao.stream.remote/v1` claim is checked, not declared: a cell\n    61\t   is published only after its kept cursor survives a round trip\n    62\t   through the channel codec -- encoded, decoded back, equal -- which\n    63\t   is what plain data in the channel's portable domain means\n    64\t   (S7.4.3; `dao.stream.remote.md` S2.3's plain-data basis).\n    65\t   `cursor-codec` is the default codec; a composition whose channel\n    66\t   speaks another passes its own `{:encode f :decode f}` to\n    67\t   `lift-pending` or `lift-frame`. A cursor that does not round trip\n    68\t   -- a host object inside is the case S2.3 names -- refuses the lift\n    69\t   as `:yin.k/unsatisfied` before any value is minted.\n    70\t\n    71\t   Every pending variant of S7.4.3 lifts and lowers: `:next`, `:put`,\n    72\t   `:ffi`, `:ffi-request`, and the linker's `:link-request`,\n    73\t   `:link-response` and `:install` (`yin.vm.linker.md` S7.2-7.3).\n    74\t   `:install` names no stream and passes through unchanged.\n    75\t\n    76\t   Scope: `lift-frame` and `lower-frame` are the frame-level entry\n    77\t   points -- cells are minted and remapped across the WHOLE entry\n    78\t   set, so two waiters on one engine cursor cell lift to one UCF\n    79\t   cell and lower back into one shared cursor entry, and independent\n    80\t   cells stay independent (S7.5.3, and the sharing rule of the S7.11\n    81\t   pending-state blocker). `lift-pending` and `lower-pending` lift or\n    82\t   lower one entry into a self-contained table.\"\n    83\t  (:require [dao.stream :as stream]\n    84\t            [dao.stream.apply :as apply2]\n    85\t            [dao.stream.cbor :as cbor]\n    86\t            [yin.vm :as vm]\n    87\t            [yin.vm.module :as module]))\n    88\t\n    89\t\n    90\t(def cursor-profiles\n    91\t  \"The one portable cursor profile S7.4.3 names in :yin.k/requires: a\n    92\t   cursor is plain data in the channel's portable domain and the\n    93\t   source honors it after serialization, cursor identity, position and\n    94\t   `gap` outcomes included.\"\n    95\t  #{:dao.stream.remote/v1})\n    96\t\n    97\t\n    98\t(def cursor-codec\n    99\t  \"The channel codec a lift round-trips kept cursors through when the\n   100\t   composition supplies none: the DaoStream v2 wire codec, whose\n   101\t   portable domain is the domain the profile claim names.\"\n   102\t  {:encode cbor/encode, :decode cbor/decode})\n   103\t\n   104\t\n   105\t(def ^:private requires\n   106\t  {:yin.k/cursor-profiles cursor-profiles})\n   107\t\n   108\t\n   109\t;; =============================================================================\n   110\t;; Markers (S7.5.1), through a remote descriptor\n   111\t;; =============================================================================\n   112\t\n   113\t(defn- local-identity\n   114\t  \"The identity `h` itself declares, when its descriptor answers ok;\n   115\t   nil otherwise -- the best name a refusal can give when `serve!` has\n   116\t   not minted one.\"\n   117\t  [h]\n   118\t  (let [d (when (stream/descriptor? h) (stream/descriptor h))]\n   119\t    (when (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome d))\n   120\t      (:dao.stream/identity d))))\n   121\t\n   122\t\n   123\t(defn- unsatisfied\n   124\t  ([] (unsatisfied nil))\n   125\t  ([identity]\n   126\t   (cond-> {:yin.k/status :yin.k/unsatisfied}\n   127\t     (some? identity) (assoc :dao.stream/identity identity))))\n   128\t\n   129\t\n   130\t(defn- portable-cursor\n   131\t  \"The kept cursor when it survives a round trip through the channel\n   132\t   codec -- encoded, decoded back, and equal -- which is what the\n   133\t   `:dao.stream.remote/v1` claim means: plain data in the channel's\n   134\t   portable domain, honored after serialization. nil when it does\n   135\t   not, so no cell is ever published over an unpublishable cursor.\"\n   136\t  [codec cursor]\n   137\t  (try (let [w (:encode codec)\n   138\t             b (w cursor)\n   139\t             r ((:decode codec) b)]\n   140\t         (when (= cursor r) cursor))\n   141\t       (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _ nil)))\n   142\t\n   143\t\n   144\t(defn lift-marker\n   145\t  \"The UCF stream marker (S7.5.1) for local handle `h`, its descriptor\n   146\t   the remote descriptor `serve!` mints by entering `h` in the\n   147\t   exporter's table (`dao.stream.remote.md` S2.2). nil when `serve!`\n   148\t   refuses -- the source cannot be entered, or cannot honor the\n   149\t   profile -- so a caller mints `:yin.k/unsatisfied` before any value\n   150\t   is produced (S7.4.3).\"\n   151\t  [serve! h]\n   152\t  (when-let [served (serve! h)]\n   153\t    (let [id (:dao.stream/identity served)]\n   154\t      {:yin.k/tag :yin.k/stream,\n   155\t       :dao.stream/identity id,\n   156\t       :dao.stream/descriptor\n   157\t       {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/remote,\n   158\t        :dao.stream/identity id,\n   159\t        :dao.stream/channel (:dao.stream/channel served)}})))\n   160\t\n   161\t\n   162\t(defn lower-marker\n   163\t  \"The local handle `attach!` gives for UCF stream marker `m` -- a\n   164\t   reflection on the original logical stream -- or nil when the attach\n   165\t   does not answer ok: a reflection marked gone, or any other refusal,\n   166\t   is unsatisfied at the caller.\"\n   167\t  [attach! m]\n   168\t  (let [result (attach! (:dao.stream/descriptor m))]\n   169\t    (when (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome result))\n   170\t      (:dao.stream/handle result))))\n   171\t\n   172\t\n   173\t;; =============================================================================\n   174\t;; The pending wait (S7.4.3)\n   175\t;; =============================================================================\n   176\t;;\n   177\t;; Local pending-entry shapes this namespace lifts, and lowers back to\n   178\t;; -- the engine's own wait-set shapes, plain data resolved through\n   179\t;; the machine's `:resources` table:\n   180\t;;\n   181\t;;   {:reason :next          :cursor-ref {:type :cursor-ref, :id cid}\n   182\t;;                           :stream-id sid}          ; blocked read\n   183\t;;   {:reason :next          :call-id id :op op        ; a sent call's\n   184\t;;                           :cursor-ref {:type :cursor-ref, :id cid}\n   185\t;;                           :stream-id sid}          ; response wait\n   186\t;;   {:reason :put           :stream-id sid            ; blocked write,\n   187\t;;                           :datom v}                 ; :datom the retry\n   188\t;;   {:reason :put           :request-sent true        ; a retained FFI\n   189\t;;                           :call-id id :op op        ; request; the\n   190\t;;                           :stream-id sid :datom env}; fixed call-in\n   191\t;;   {:reason :link-request  :link-id id :name m :envelope e\n   192\t;;                           :request rid :response rid' :cursor kept}\n   193\t;;   {:reason :link-response :link-id id :name m\n   194\t;;                           :request rid :response rid' :cursor kept}\n   195\t;;   {:reason :install       :name m}\n   196\t;;\n   197\t;; `cid` names a `:resources` cursor entry `{:stream-id sid :cursor\n   198\t;; kept}` (`vm/cursor-entry`); `sid` and `rid` name stream handles in\n   199\t;; the same table; `env` is the retained `dao.stream.apply` envelope.\n   200\t;; On a real entry the retained request's `sid` is the engine's fixed\n   201\t;; `vm/call-in-stream-key`, and a link wait's `rid`/`rid'` are the\n   202\t;; fixed `module/link-request-resource` and\n   203\t;; `module/link-response-resource` -- the keys the engine's own retry\n   204\t;; and poll paths resolve.\n   205\t;; The registers every parked entry also carries -- :segment :pc :env\n   206\t;; :stack :k -- are the machine's own context: the lift ignores them\n   207\t;; (they travel in the frame's continuation slice, not the pending\n   208\t;; wait), and the lower stamps the caller's context back onto the\n   209\t;; entry it builds.\n   210\t\n   211\t\n   212\t;; =============================================================================\n   213\t;; Cell minting and remapping (S7.5.3), across the whole frame set\n   214\t;; =============================================================================\n   215\t\n   216\t(defn- mint-cell\n   217\t  \"One mint per distinct cursor cell across the WHOLE frame set:\n   218\t   `found` accumulates the remapping {cell key -> UCF cell id} beside\n   219\t   the cells themselves as entries lift, so two waiters on one\n   220\t   engine cursor cell lift to one UCF cell, and independent cells\n   221\t   stay independent, whatever their positions (S7.5.3). `k` is the\n   222\t   cell's identity -- the engine cursor resource id a `:next` waiter\n   223\t   names, or the response stream and kept cursor a link wait names.\n   224\t   The cell carries the marker `serve!` mints for the cell's stream\n   225\t   and its kept cursor, published only after the cursor survives the\n   226\t   codec round trip; nil -- refuse -- when it does not, or when\n   227\t   `serve!` refuses the stream.\"\n   228\t  [serve! found]\n   229\t  (fn [codec k handle kept]\n   230\t    (or (get-in @found [:of k])\n   231\t        (let [portable (portable-cursor codec kept)\n   232\t              marker (when portable (lift-marker serve! handle))]\n   233\t          (when (and portable marker)\n   234\t            (let [cid (keyword \"yin.k\" (str \"c-\" (count (:cells @found))))\n   235\t                  cell {:yin.k/stream marker, :yin.k/position portable}]\n   236\t              (swap! found #(-> % (assoc-in [:of k] cid)\n   237\t                                (assoc-in [:cells cid] cell)))\n   238\t              cid))))))\n   239\t\n   240\t\n   241\t(defn- cursor-cell\n   242\t  \"The engine cursor cell a `:next` entry waits on: `:cursor-ref`'s\n   243\t   id into `:resources`, the cursor entry carrying the stream id and\n   244\t   the kept cursor (`vm/cursor-entry`). nil when it names nothing.\"\n   245\t  [resources entry]\n   246\t  (let [cid (get-in entry [:cursor-ref :id])\n   247\t        cell (get resources cid)]\n   248\t    (when (and (some? cid) (map? cell) (contains? cell :stream-id))\n   249\t      {:id cid, :stream-id (:stream-id cell), :cursor (:cursor cell)})))\n   250\t\n   251\t\n   252\t(defn- cell-identity\n   253\t  \"The stream identity a `:yin.k/cells` entry names, for a refusal\n   254\t   that names it even when the cell itself never lowered.\"\n   255\t  [cells cell-id]\n   256\t  (get-in cells [cell-id :yin.k/stream :dao.stream/identity]))\n   257\t\n   258\t\n   259\t;; =============================================================================\n   260\t;; Lift: one variant each\n   261\t;; =============================================================================\n   262\t\n   263\t(defn- lift-read\n   264\t  \"S7.4.3's blocked read: the cell `mint` gives for the entry's\n   265\t   cursor cell, at its kept position.\"\n   266\t  [resources mint codec entry]\n   267\t  (if-let [cell (cursor-cell resources entry)]\n   268\t    (if-let [cid (mint codec (:id cell)\n   269\t                       (get resources (:stream-id cell))\n   270\t                       (:cursor cell))]\n   271\t      {:pending {:yin.k/reason :next, :yin.k/cell cid}, :cell? true}\n   272\t      (unsatisfied (local-identity (get resources (:stream-id cell)))))\n   273\t    (unsatisfied)))\n   274\t\n   275\t\n   276\t(defn- lift-response-wait\n   277\t  \"S7.4.3's sent call: a `:next` entry carrying `:call-id`, the\n   278\t   response wait `semantic-restore` rebuilds -- the call's\n   279\t   correlation beside the response cell at its kept position, so\n   280\t   round-order on the cell is preserved on the resumer (S7.4.3).\n   640\t                         (= :link-request reason)\n   641\t                         (assoc :envelope (:yin.k/envelope pending))))}\n   642\t        (unsatisfied (cell-identity cells cid))))\n   643\t\n   644\t    :install\n   645\t    {:entry (merge ctx\n   646\t                   {:reason :install, :name (:yin.k/name pending)})}\n   647\t\n   648\t    {:yin.k/status :yin.k/undecodable,\n   649\t     :yin.k/reason (:yin.k/reason pending)}))\n   650\t\n   651\t\n   652\t(defn lower-frame\n   653\t  \"A whole frame set of S7.4.3 pendings, lowered together:\n   654\t   `{:entries [e ...], :resources table}` -- one fresh stream entry\n   655\t   per distinct served identity and one fresh cursor entry per\n   656\t   distinct UCF cell, two waiters on one cell lowering back into one\n   657\t   shared entry and independent cells staying independent (S7.5.3) --\n   658\t   with the same `:yin.k/unsatisfied` and `:yin.k/undecodable`\n   659\t   outcomes `lift-frame` gives. Where the engine resolves a wait at\n   660\t   one of its own fixed resource keys, the lower parks the wait\n   661\t   there: a link wait's pair under `module/link-request-resource` and\n   662\t   `module/link-response-resource`, and a retained request as the\n   663\t   engine's own `:request-sent` shape on the fixed local call-in key\n   664\t   -- the receiver's own pair serves the retried request and the\n   665\t   restored response wait reads the receiver's own call-out, while\n   666\t   every response route lowers under its cell's own fresh key, never\n   667\t   at a fixed key. `contexts`, parallel to `pendings`, is each\n   668\t   entry's register context, merged verbatim.\"\n   669\t  [attach! pendings cells contexts]\n   670\t  (let [ms (distinct (remove nil?\n   671\t                             (concat (map :yin.k/stream (vals cells))\n   672\t                                     (mapcat pending-markers pendings))))\n   673\t        a (attach-all attach! ms)]\n   674\t    (if (contains? a :yin.k/status)\n   675\t      a\n   676\t      (let [lowered (mapv #(lower-one %1 %2 (:streams a) cells)\n   677\t                          pendings contexts)]\n   678\t        (if-let [refusal (some #(when (contains? % :yin.k/status) %)\n   679\t                               lowered)]\n   680\t          refusal\n   681\t          {:entries (mapv :entry lowered),\n   682\t           :resources (merge (:resources a)\n   683\t                             (cell-resources (:streams a) cells)\n   684\t                             (link-pair-resources (:streams a)\n   685\t                                                  (:resources a)\n   686\t                                                  pendings))})))))\n   687\t\n   688\t\n   689\t(defn lower-pending\n   690\t  \"One pending entry of S7.4.3, lowered: `{:entry e, :resources\n   691\t   table}` -- the wait-set entry `ctx`'s registers ride on, and the\n   692\t   fresh resource entries it polls through -- with the same\n   693\t   `:yin.k/unsatisfied` and `:yin.k/undecodable` outcomes\n   694\t   `lower-frame` gives. Frame assembly that shares cursor entries\n   695\t   across entries is `lower-frame`'s.\"\n   696\t  [attach! pending cells ctx]\n   697\t  (let [r (lower-frame attach! [pending] cells [ctx])]\n   698\t    (if (contains? r :yin.k/status)\n   699\t      r\n   700\t      (-> r\n   701\t          (assoc :entry (first (:entries r)))\n   702\t          (dissoc :entries)))))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
