{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '600,635p;785,802p;915,952p;1095,1118p'\"","aggregated_output":"   600\t  surface, preserving its outcome maps and cursor namespace rather than\n   601\t  assigning new positions. The cell carries its kept cursor. The receiver\n   602\t  attaches a reflection through that descriptor and resumes at the kept\n   603\t  cursor. `:yin.k/cursor-profiles` in `:yin.k/requires` names\n   604\t  `:dao.stream.remote/v1`, the one profile: a cursor is plain data in the\n   605\t  channel's portable domain and the source honors it after\n   606\t  serialization, including cursor identity, position, and `gap` outcomes.\n   607\t  If the source cannot be entered in a table, preserve the cursor, or\n   608\t  keep a channel reachable, lift refuses as `:yin.k/unsatisfied` before\n   609\t  minting the value. A reflection marked gone on the receiver is also\n   610\t  `:yin.k/unsatisfied`, naming the stream identity. The exporter keeps\n   611\t  the entry served under a lease the resumer holds\n   612\t  (`dao.stream.remote.md`, section 6). Re-homing a stream is out of\n   613\t  scope: serving from the exporter pins the exporter.\n   614\t- **Outstanding calls route to the emitter's pair; the resumer's pair is its\n   615\t  own.** The reference machine restores a `:request-sent` entry into a\n   616\t  response wait through its fixed local store keys (`vm/call-out-stream-key`\n   617\t  and friends). A resumer must *not* do that: the outstanding call's\n   618\t  response will arrive on the **emitter's** response stream, because that is\n   619\t  where the request was sent. The resumer re-establishes the wait against\n   620\t  the carried response descriptor and the cell's fresh local key (the cell\n   621\t  id remapped per §7.5.3), while calls the resumed program makes *after*\n   622\t  lowering use the resumer's own pair under its own keys. Pending-call\n   623\t  routing and future-call routing are separate stores of state; conflating\n   624\t  them strands the migrated call exactly as the review described.\n   625\t- **On resume, the resumer attaches and re-mints.** Attach to every\n   626\t  descriptor in the frame, pending, and cells; an attachment resolving to\n   627\t  `not-found` is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream identity, never a\n   628\t  silent `nil`; a `:put` wait retries its retained value and resumes with it\n   629\t  on `ok` (the engine, a woken writer's retry appends and stamps the\n   630\t  value), where through a reflection `ok` is acceptance on the outbound\n   631\t  path and the wait resumes only when the source's `ok` is observed on the\n   632\t  link's event writer, while an append whose effect is unknown leaves the\n   633\t  wait undischarged for the program or its composition to decide\n   634\t  (`dao.stream.remote.md`, sections 2.4 and 2.5); a `:next` wait polls\n   635\t  its cell. No wait is silently dropped and no\n   785\tlower into these shapes, the walker's included, with its\n   786\t`:unrooted-body` refusal. The store named by `:yin.k/store-of` travels\n   787\tonce per slice, not per closure, under the module-store amendment of\n   788\tsection 7.6.2.\n   789\t\n   790\t**Resource markers decode into a private table, and references re-seal\n   791\t(linker M4).** Specified by `yin.vm.linker.md` and implemented in M4. The\n   792\t`:yin.k/stream` and `:yin.k/cursor-ref` rows above decode to a store key\n   793\tand a store cursor entry; the linker's resource split moves both targets\n   794\tout of the store (`yin.vm.linker.md` 7.3). Lowering installs an attached\n   795\thandle under a fresh resource id in the receiver's private `:resources`\n   796\ttable, and lowers a logical cell to a cursor entry in `:resources` seeded\n   797\twith its carried position, remapping every `:yin.k/cursor-ref` to that\n   798\tentry -- two refs to one cell still share one entry -- and no store key is\n   799\tever created: program values keep only the opaque `stream-ref` and\n   800\t`cursor-ref` ids, exactly as a running program holds them. References are\n   801\tsealed, not merely shaped. Each task's resources are bound to a\n   802\ttask-scoped capability secret, minted once by the composition at task\n   915\t\n   916\t**Cursor cells.** A cursor is *observable state with aliasing*: two distinct\n   917\tcursor refs may sit at the same position and yet be independently\n   918\tadvanceable store entries, while repeated refs to one cursor ref must\n   919\tadvance together — `check-wait-set` advances a shared cursor ref before\n   920\tpolling the next waiter, so waiters on one ref read successive values, and\n   921\twaiters on two refs at the same position read the same next value\n   922\t(`yin.vm.engine`, `check-wait-set`). A position-only encoding erases\n   923\texactly this distinction: two cells at one position would collapse, and\n   924\ttheir independent advancement would be lost. The encoding therefore carries\n   925\t**logical cursor cells** separately from position content:\n   926\t\n   927\t```clojure\n   928\t:yin.k/cells {:yin.k/c-17 {:yin.k/stream   {:yin.k/tag :yin.k/stream …}\n   929\t                           :yin.k/position <opaque, exactly as the transport minted it>}}\n   930\t;; values reference cells, never positions:\n   931\t{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/cursor-ref :yin.k/cell :yin.k/c-17}\n   932\t```\n   933\t\n   934\tCell ids are minted by the emitter (stable within one lift; remapped on\n   935\tlowering) and are *not* content-addressed — a cell is a local identity, like\n   936\ta tempid, and deduplicating cells by their contents is precisely the aliasing\n   937\terasure this structure exists to prevent. On lowering, each cell becomes a\n   938\tfresh store cursor entry seeded with its carried position; every\n   939\t`:yin.k/cursor-ref` to it remaps to that entry; two refs to one cell share\n   940\tone entry and one ref per cell keeps its own. The FFI response cell of\n   941\t§7.4.3 is a cell like any other.\n   942\t\n   943\t**Cells lower into the private resource table (linker M4).** Specified by\n   944\t`yin.vm.linker.md` and implemented in M4. The lowering rule above -- each\n   945\tcell becomes a fresh store cursor entry -- is amended by the resource\n   946\tsplit of `yin.vm.linker.md` 7.3: each cell becomes a cursor entry in the\n   947\treceiver's private `:resources` table, seeded with its carried position,\n   948\tand every `:yin.k/cursor-ref` to it remaps to that entry; two refs to one\n   949\tcell still share one entry and one ref per cell keeps its own, but no\n   950\tstore key is ever created, and program values keep only the opaque\n   951\treference ids, exactly as a running program holds them.\n   952\t\n  1095\tThe ids may stay predictable: they name nothing a store instruction can\n  1096\treach. A migration slice still carries resource cells, remapped at resume,\n  1097\tbut beside the program store; the decode targets of sections 7.5.1 and\n  1098\t7.5.3 move with them.\n  1099\t\n  1100\t**There is no merge.** The first draft said, in two places, both that the\n  1101\tslice wins on collision and that the slice merges *under* the local store —\n  1102\ta contradiction, and beneath it a real defect: resolution is env → store →\n  1103\tprimitives (`resolve-var`), so a resumer whose local store happens to bind\n  1104\t`+` would change what the migrated program resolves, without any collision\n  1105\tat all. Merging in either order is the wrong shape. **The resumed task runs\n  1106\tin an isolated execution store**: a fresh store whose contents are exactly\n  1107\tthe carried slice, the remapped cells of §7.5.3, and the resumer's FFI pair\n  1108\t— nothing else of the resumer's, nothing of any other task's. A name absent\n  1109\tfrom the slice resolves through primitives and modules at the resumer\n  1110\texactly as it did at the emitter, because there is no third store to\n  1111\tintervene; a slice key cannot collide with a receiver task's key because no\n  1112\treceiver key participates; resource entries are remapped to fresh local\n  1113\tkeys (cursor cells per §7.5.3, stream handles per §7.4.3), and non-resource\n  1114\tentries keep their keys verbatim. \"Unreachable store entries do not travel\"\n  1115\tsurvives unchanged — anything the program did not observe is not the\n  1116\tprogram's state — and nothing else shares state with the resumed task by\n  1117\taccident.\n  1118\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
